WEBVTT

NOTE On-screen visual text only; this approved video has no audio track.

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.350
A units wheel represents nine and a tens wheel represents zero.

2
00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:14.517
Adding one unit returns the first wheel to zero and advances the second to one.

3
00:00:14.517 --> 00:00:21.867
The mechanism represents ten without requiring a person to remember the carry.

4
00:00:22.367 --> 00:00:29.217
A pattern of holes encodes instructions for selecting positions.

5
00:00:29.217 --> 00:00:36.067
A second card activates another sequence without rebuilding the mechanism.

6
00:00:36.067 --> 00:00:43.417
Separating instructions from execution lets different procedures be repeated.

7
00:00:43.917 --> 00:00:50.767
Two switches in series must both close to complete the path.

8
00:00:50.767 --> 00:00:57.617
If A or B remains open, the output is zero.

9
00:00:57.617 --> 00:01:04.967
When both close, the output is one: the circuit implements A AND B.

10
00:01:05.467 --> 00:01:12.317
The tape contains three ones followed by an empty cell.

11
00:01:12.317 --> 00:01:19.167
The rule moves right while it reads a one.

12
00:01:19.167 --> 00:01:27.217
At the empty cell it writes another one and stops, incrementing a unary count.

13
00:01:27.717 --> 00:01:34.567
Memory contains load, add and store instructions alongside two data values.

14
00:01:34.567 --> 00:01:41.417
The execution unit reads one instruction at a time and updates its register.

15
00:01:41.417 --> 00:01:48.767
In this example it loads three, adds four and stores seven.

16
00:01:49.267 --> 00:01:56.117
A message can be A, B, C or D, each equally likely.

17
00:01:56.117 --> 00:02:02.967
Two binary decisions distinguish the four possibilities.

18
00:02:02.967 --> 00:02:10.317
Bits quantify uncertainty in this choice, not the message’s meaning.

